       1	# Stage-3 proof: Baffet-type 2D energy for a matched Maxwell-Debye PML
       2	
       3	Problem id: `2d_maxwell_pml_energy_functional_exploration`
       4	
       5	Task: `task-stage3-final-report`
       6	
       7	## Purpose
       8	
       9	The previous stage-3 artifact proves that the standard stretched-coordinate
      10	Maxwell-Debye PML is perfectly matched, but only gives a monotone energy in the
      11	one-dimensional normal-incidence block.  This artifact records a full two
      12	dimensional constant-coefficient Baffet-type formulation for the same stretched
      13	coordinate model.  The formulation uses a time primitive of the electric field
      14	and a time primitive of the Debye polarization, which is exactly the type of
      15	augmented/time-integrated variable used in Baffet-type PML energy arguments.
      16	
      17	## Assumptions and notation
      18	
      19	Let
      20	\[
      21	e=\epsilon_0\epsilon_\infty>0,\quad \mu>0,\quad \chi>0,\quad \tau>0.
      22	\]
      23	In the Laplace domain,
      24	\[
      25	\varepsilon_D(s)=e+\frac{\chi}{1+\tau s}.
      26	\]
      27	On one constant-coefficient PML block let
      28	\[
      29	\sigma_x,\sigma_y\ge 0,\qquad
      30	a=\sigma_x+\sigma_y,\qquad b=\sigma_x\sigma_y,
      31	\]
      32	and assume \(a>0\) for the PML block.  The undamped physical block \(a=b=0\)
      33	uses the ordinary Maxwell-Debye energy and is coupled by the usual transmission
      34	flux.  Define
      35	\[
      36	\Gamma_1=\begin{pmatrix}\sigma_x&0\\0&\sigma_y\end{pmatrix},\qquad
      37	\Gamma_2=\begin{pmatrix}\sigma_y-\sigma_x&0\\0&\sigma_x-\sigma_y\end{pmatrix}.
      38	\]
      39	For a vector \(v\), write \(|v|_M^2=v\cdot Mv\).
      40	
      41	Let \(u\) be the time primitive of the electric field:
      42	\[
      43	E=u_t.
      44	\]
      45	Let \(q\) be the time primitive of the Debye polarization:
      46	\[
      47	q_t=P.
      48	\]
      49	The Debye law is written in compatible primitive form
      50	\[
      51	\tau P_t+P=\chi u_t,\qquad q+\tau P=\chi u.
      52	\]
      53	The second relation is preserved by the first two equations if it is true at
      54	initial time.
      55	
      56	## Baffet-type matched PML system
      57	
      58	Introduce the Baffet auxiliary vector \(\phi=(\phi_x,\phi_y)\) and
      59	\[
      60	\lambda=\nabla u+\phi.
      61	\]
      62	The constant-coefficient two-dimensional repaired model is
      63	\[
      64	\boxed{
      65	\mu\{e(u_{tt}+a u_t+b u)+P_t+aP+bq\}-\nabla\cdot\lambda=0,}
      66	\tag{1}
      67	\]
      68	\[
      69	\boxed{\phi_t+\Gamma_1\phi=\Gamma_2\nabla u,}
      70	\tag{2}
      71	\]
      72	\[
      73	\boxed{\tau P_t+P=\chi u_t,\qquad q_t=P,\qquad q+\tau P=\chi u.}
      74	\tag{3}
      75	\]
      76	Outer boundary conditions are taken so that the boundary flux
      77	\[
      78	\int_{\partial\Omega}(u_t+au)\lambda\cdot n\,ds
      79	\]
      80	is zero or nonpositive.  For a PEC outer boundary, the compatible primitive
      81	condition is \(u=0\), hence \(u_t=E=0\), on that boundary.  On piecewise
      82	constant internal interfaces, the usual transmission conditions are continuity
      83	of \(u\) and \(\lambda\cdot n\), so the flux cancels pairwise.
      84	
      85	## Equivalence with the stretched-coordinate Maxwell-Debye PML
      86	
      87	For constant coefficients, Laplace transforming (2) gives
      88	\[
      89	\widehat\phi_x=\frac{\sigma_y-\sigma_x}{s+\sigma_x}\partial_x\widehat u,
      90	\qquad
      91	\widehat\phi_y=\frac{\sigma_x-\sigma_y}{s+\sigma_y}\partial_y\widehat u.
      92	\]
      93	Thus
      94	\[
      95	\widehat\lambda_x
      96	=\frac{s+\sigma_y}{s+\sigma_x}\partial_x\widehat u
      97	=\frac{S_y}{S_x}\partial_x\widehat u,\qquad
      98	\widehat\lambda_y
      99	=\frac{s+\sigma_x}{s+\sigma_y}\partial_y\widehat u
     100	=\frac{S_x}{S_y}\partial_y\widehat u,
     101	\]
     102	where
     103	\[
     104	S_i(s)=1+\frac{\sigma_i}{s}=\frac{s+\sigma_i}{s}.
     105	\]
     106	The Debye compatibility gives
     107	\[
     108	\widehat P=\frac{\chi s}{1+\tau s}\widehat u,\qquad
     109	\widehat q=\frac{1}{s}\widehat P.
     110	\]
     111	Therefore
     112	\[
     113	e(s^2+as+b)\widehat u+s\widehat P+a\widehat P+b\widehat q
     114	=\left(e+\frac{\chi}{1+\tau s}\right)(s^2+as+b)\widehat u
     115	=\varepsilon_D(s)s^2S_xS_y\widehat u.
     116	\]
     117	Equation (1) becomes
     118	\[
     119	\mu\varepsilon_D(s)s^2S_xS_y\widehat u
     120	=\nabla\cdot\widehat\lambda.
     121	\]
     122	Dividing by \(S_xS_y\) gives
     123	\[
     124	\mu\varepsilon_D(s)s^2\widehat u
     125	=S_x^{-1}\partial_x(S_x^{-1}\partial_x\widehat u)
     126	+S_y^{-1}\partial_y(S_y^{-1}\partial_y\widehat u).
     127	\]
     128	Since \(\widehat E=s\widehat u\), this is exactly the second-order equation
     129	obtained by eliminating \(H_x,H_y\) from the standard stretched-coordinate
     130	Maxwell-Debye PML while keeping
     131	\[
     132	(1+\tau s)\widehat P=\chi \widehat E.
     133	\]
     134	Consequently this Baffet-type time-domain system has the same planar-interface
     135	perfect matching as the standard stretched-coordinate PML: at an \(x\)-interface
     136	with \(S_y=1\), the normal symbol satisfies \(\nu=S_x\nu_0\) and the tangential
     137	Maxwell admittance is unchanged.
     138	
     139	## Energy functional
     140	
     141	Define
     142	\[
     143	\alpha=1+2a\tau,\qquad
     144	\beta=a+b\tau,\qquad
     145	\gamma=a^2+b(1+a\tau).
     146	\]
     147	The Baffet-Debye energy is
     148	\[
     149	\boxed{
     150	\begin{aligned}
     151	\mathcal E_{BD}
     152	=\frac12\int_\Omega \{&
     153	\mu e(u_t+a u)^2+|\lambda|^2
     154	+|\phi|_{a^{-1}\Gamma_1}^2+\mu e\,b u^2\\
     155	&+\frac{\mu}{\chi}\left(\alpha P^2+2\beta Pq+\gamma q^2\right)
     156	\}\,dxdy .
     157	\end{aligned}}
     158	\tag{4}
     159	\]
     160	The \(P,q\) block is positive definite whenever \(a>0\).  Indeed
     161	\[
     162	\alpha>0,\qquad
     163	\alpha\gamma-\beta^2
     164	=b+ab\tau+2a^3\tau+b(2a^2-b)\tau^2>0,
     165	\]
     166	because \(a=\sigma_x+\sigma_y>0\), \(b=\sigma_x\sigma_y\ge0\), \(\tau>0\), and
     167	\(2a^2-b\ge0\).  Thus (4) is nonnegative, and it is coercive on the PML block
     168	modulo the usual zero modes of the primitive variables and boundary conditions.
     169	
     170	## Energy identity
     171	
     172	Test (1) with \(u_t+a u\).  The nondispersive part gives the usual Baffet
     173	calculation:
     174	\[
     175	\frac12\frac{d}{dt}\int_\Omega
     176	\mu e\{(u_t+a u)^2+b u^2\}\,dxdy
     177	+\int_\Omega \mu e\,ab u^2\,dxdy
     178	=\int_\Omega (u_t+a u)\nabla\cdot\lambda\,dxdy
     179	-\mu\int_\Omega (P_t+aP+bq)(u_t+a u)\,dxdy .
     180	\tag{5}
     181	\]
     182	The boundary part of the first term is the flux stated above, and the interior
     183	part is handled by the same Baffet multiplier used for \(\lambda\).  Specifically,
     184	from
     185	\[
     186	\lambda_t+\Gamma_1\lambda=\nabla u_t+\widetilde\Gamma_2\nabla u,\qquad
     187	\widetilde\Gamma_2=\Gamma_2+\Gamma_1
     188	=\begin{pmatrix}\sigma_y&0\\0&\sigma_x\end{pmatrix},
     189	\]
     190	and the multiplier
     191	\[
     192	g=(I+a^{-1}\Gamma_1)\lambda-a^{-1}\Gamma_1\nabla u,
     193	\]
     194	one obtains
     195	\[
     196	\begin{aligned}
     197	&\frac12\frac{d}{dt}\int_\Omega
     198	\left(|\lambda|^2+|\phi|_{a^{-1}\Gamma_1}^2\right)\,dxdy\\
     199	&\quad+\int_\Omega
     200	\left(|\lambda|^2_{\Gamma_1(I+a^{-1}\Gamma_1)}
     201	+\frac{b}{a}|\nabla u|^2\right)\,dxdy
     202	=\int_\Omega \nabla(u_t+a u)\cdot\lambda\,dxdy .
     203	\end{aligned}
     204	\tag{6}
     205	\]
     206	This is the two-dimensional Baffet identity; it uses
     207	\(\Gamma_1\widetilde\Gamma_2=bI\) and
     208	\(a^{-1}\Gamma_1(\Gamma_1+\widetilde\Gamma_2)+\widetilde\Gamma_2=aI\).
     209	
     210	It remains to compute the Debye primitive contribution.  Using
     211	\[
     212	u_t+a u
     213	=\frac{\tau P_t+(1+a\tau)P+a q}{\chi},
     214	\]
     215	we have
     216	\[
     217	\begin{aligned}
     218	&(P_t+aP+bq)(u_t+a u)\\
     219	&=\frac1\chi\Big[
     220	\frac12\frac{d}{dt}
     221	\left(\alpha P^2+2\beta Pq+\gamma q^2\right)
     222	+\tau P_t^2+\tau(a^2-b)P^2+abq^2
     223	\Big].
     224	\end{aligned}
     225	\tag{7}
     226	\]
     227	Here \(q_t=P\), and the constants \(\alpha,\beta,\gamma\) are exactly those in
     228	(4).
     229	
     230	Adding (5), (6), and (7) gives the full identity

